WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:08.240
David, do you think that Trump policies are&nbsp;
actually more popular than Republican policies?&nbsp;

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:12.720
This is an interesting question, because a lot of&nbsp;
Trump policies aren't really policies. And this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:16.480
one of the important things to understand&nbsp;
that gets us back to our discussion about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:24.240
the allure, the sometimes deceptive allure&nbsp;
of populist rhetoric, which then implodes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:28.560
in either authoritarianism or incompetence&nbsp;
or in the case of Donald Trump, both.&nbsp;

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:36.400
There are lots of, quote, Republican policies that&nbsp;
are not very popular anymore. If you look at just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:42.720
to pick something, if you look at the topic of&nbsp;
abortion, the Republican Party continues to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:50.560
among its elected officials, very anti choice.
But the U.S. population has moved more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:55.680
and more to the left in terms of&nbsp;
believing that abortion should be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:03.120
legal and safe in most cases. So in that sense,&nbsp;
that's a Republican policy that's not very popular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:09.840
with the American people. The tricky and sort of&nbsp;
pernicious thing about Donald Trump's policies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:15.040
is that many of them are actually policies.&nbsp;
They're just. Vague promises that are meant to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:22.240
play on the resentment and discontent of large&nbsp;
swaths of Americans. So I'll give you an example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:28.400
When Donald Trump ran on, we're getting screwed on&nbsp;
trade. You're getting screwed and you're getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:31.760
screwed whether you're a Democrat or a Republican.&nbsp;
Even if he wasn't saying that everybody's getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:36.240
screwed and China this in China that I&nbsp;
understand that and I'm going to fix it.&nbsp;

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:45.840
That's not a policy, but it accurately sort&nbsp;
of rang a bell of discontent and resentment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:51.760
that many people had, and in that sense, it was a&nbsp;
popular thing for him to say we can't really call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:58.320
it a popular policy because it's not actually a&nbsp;
policy. And as we saw when Donald Trump actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:03.840
negotiated that trade deal and said, we fixed&nbsp;
everything, we fixed NAFTA, it was really NAFTA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:09.520
redux with a different name and a lot of the same&nbsp;
elements. The policy itself was not actually any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:15.040
better than what we had before. So I want to be&nbsp;
really careful about saying Trump's policies are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:19.520
more popular than Republican policies because many&nbsp;
of them aren't actually policies. Another example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:29.200
health care. Donald Trump got some favorable&nbsp;
ratings while he was running for president&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:34.080
on the idea of getting rid of Obamacare and&nbsp;
replacing it with something that's going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:38.240
be great and we're going to keep coverage for&nbsp;
preexisting conditions. There are people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:43.040
certainly like that and they arguably liked it&nbsp;
as well or better than the Republican proposals.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:50.160
But there was really nothing there.
And once the actual proposal was put forward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:55.120
and it was scored and it was determined that&nbsp;
20, 30 million people would end up without care,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.120 --> 00:03:00.080
who currently had it didn't go anywhere.
And to even call it a policy is a bit of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:04.800
stretch. And then you have things that are sort&nbsp;
of more pedestrian. I mean, a lot of the things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:10.240
Trump wanted were not popular. Trump was widely&nbsp;
against, you know, covid mask mandate or really a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:15.440
request from the presidency. Joe Biden did that.&nbsp;
It has 80 percent support. So that clearly wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:21.360
the popular thing that Donald Trump stood for.&nbsp;
Building the wall was never particularly popular.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:25.360
It was kind of right in there, split among&nbsp;
party lines. So you start with party lines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:30.160
and shift a little bit towards less popular. So&nbsp;
I think for some of these things that were more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:35.600
actual policy ideas from Trump, they weren't&nbsp;
particularly popular. The reason the Trump.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:43.440
Policy platform, if you can even call it that,&nbsp;
lulled a lot of people into supporting it, is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:48.400
it was essentially just populist rhetoric, which&nbsp;
a lot of people like, even some people on the left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:55.440
without actual policy prescriptions, which with&nbsp;
which when Trump did actually put out in detail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:00.880
were widely unpopular.
Right now, this continues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:08.240
to be a problem. The American electorate is&nbsp;
far to the left of where our candidates are.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:15.280
The American electorate is well to the left&nbsp;
of the average of the 100 senators on so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:19.840
different issues ranging from income inequality.&nbsp;
What tax rates should be all the way to social&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:26.800
issues like abortion. So a lot of Republican and&nbsp;
Trump policies aren't popular, but they continue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:35.760
to win in many ways because the wedge issues that&nbsp;
are really great fundraising issues continue to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:40.800
sort of wielded like a cudgel by many Republican&nbsp;
elected officials and candidates. And we'll have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:45.440
time to see if and how that shifts between&nbsp;
now and the twenty, twenty two elections.

